{
  "teamId": 134,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 Pittsburgh Pirates entered the year with cautious optimism, and for a brief stretch in the spring, that optimism felt justified. Kevin Newman emerged as an unlikely sparkplug, Starling Marte delivered a series of timely home runs, and the club posted a winning record through May at 28-28. It was a fragile foundation, but a foundation nonetheless — one built on individual heroics rather than the kind of sustained pitching and run prevention that separates contenders from pretenders.\n\nJune turned the tide. The bullpen, already a source of concern, began to unravel in spectacular fashion. Kyle Crick surrendered back-to-back home runs to the same Atlanta rookie on consecutive series visits, and opposing hitters feasted on a relief corps that couldn't protect leads. The Pirates went 11-15 in June, and the window for a surprising playoff push began to close. July was even more punishing — an 8-18 record that buried any remaining postseason hopes and sent the front office into seller mode at the trade deadline.\n\nThe August and September collapse was the final indictment of a roster built on thin margins. Richard Rodríguez's inability to hold late leads, Michael Feliz's repeated meltdowns, and a bullpen that collectively posted an ERA well above league average turned close games into losses with disheartening regularity. Josh Bell's breakout season — he finished with 37 home runs — provided a lone beacon of genuine star-level production, but a 5.18 team ERA meant nearly every game felt like an uphill battle.\n\nThe season's final chapter offered one last moment of irony: Kevin Newman, who had opened the year as a feel-good story, closed it with a walk-off home run on the final weekend, a reminder of what fleeting hope looks like in Pittsburgh. The 69-93 record told the real story — a franchise that allowed 911 runs, couldn't keep games close, and watched a promising April disintegrate into one of the most disappointing second halves in recent team history.",
